Thanks. We subsequently replaced the 72bcba3 shutdown fallback while rebuilding #1656 on the clean AWEX-only history, so that commit is no longer part of the current head (17c308bc).

  The current implementation handles this at the pair-ownership level:

  - shutdown claims the active controller, any in-flight candidate, and all stale controllers before  destroying rollout or training runtimes;
  - each pair first goes through its gateway /disconnect;
  - when gateway disconnect fails, the direct fallback sends /awex/teardown to every related training and  inference endpoint with:
    {"pair_name": pair_name, "operation_id": operation_id, "rpc_timeout_s": timeout};
  - teardown requests use return_exceptions=True, so one failed endpoint does not prevent cleanup attempts  for the remaining endpoints;
  - a 409/in-flight operation is retained as cleanup_pending instead of racing direct teardown against  active collective work;
  - pair identity, port reservation, and private-gateway ownership are retained until cleanup is  confirmed;
  - unresolved cleanup is surfaced to PPOTrainer.close(), which does not destroy the rollout/training  runtimes first.

  The corresponding tests cover active/candidate/stale ownership, gateway failure with direct fallback,  endpoint failure isolation, repeated cleanup, and runtime shutdown ordering.

  Resolving this thread as addressed by the unified pair-scoped cleanup path in the current head.

Done in the current clean head.

  We followed this suggestion and moved deterministic rollout recovery out of #1656. The earlier reply  about keeping it in this PR applied to the superseded mixed-scope history and is no longer accurate.

  The current head (17c308bc) contains none of the deterministic-recovery changes:

  - no AREAL_DETERMINISTIC_SAMPLING gate;
  - no checkpoint-safe/no-read-ahead dispatcher;
  - no TaskIdGenerator checkpoint state;
  - no recover_state_dict()/load_recover_state_dict() additions in either rollout frontend;
  - no pipeline_info.pkl changes;
  - no deterministic-specific workflow/recovery tests;
  - no RecoverHandler.dump() signature change.

  #1656 is now limited to V2 separated-device AWEX reconnect and recovery safety.

  A follow-up deterministic-recovery PR will use rollout.deterministic_sampling as the sole switch, share  recovery-state schema and compatibility handling between both rollout frontends, and independently  validate task-ID/dataloader continuation and the default asynchronous scheduling path.

  Resolving this thread as addressed by the scope split.

 Thanks. This is addressed in the current clean head.

  The earlier b8c23c3/a264b72 commits belonged to the superseded mixed-scope history and are no longer ancestors of the PR head. Their required ownership behavior has been retained and extended in 17c308bc:

  - Megatron and SGLang store the partially initialized candidate in pair-scoped state before attempting  rollback.
  - Payload and control-group handles are cleared independently only after their destruction succeeds.
  - A handle whose destruction fails remains reachable for a later teardown_weight_update_group(pair_name)  retry.
  - Cleanup failures do not replace the original initialization exception.
  - Initializing or cleaning candidate B does not discard the previously active pair A; A can be  reactivated without mixing its groups, rank, transfer plan, or DTE runtime with B.
  - The gateway keeps the PairInfo, KV metadata, operation record, worker URLs, and cleanup_pending status  until every worker teardown succeeds.
  - A cleanup-pending pair cannot be published as active or accept a same-name reconnect.
  - Registry, KV, and operation ownership are removed only after successful teardown.

  The fault-injection coverage includes:

  - NCCL initialization succeeds;
  - Gloo initialization fails;
  - the first NCCL destroy fails;
  - the failed handle remains owned;
  - the second teardown succeeds;
  - gateway rollback preserves bookkeeping until retry;
  - A → B → A pair reactivation remains pair-scoped.

  These are mocked process-group fault-injection tests rather than a real multi-GPU failure run.

  Resolving this thread as fixed in the current clean head.
